//? Title: Statistical methods remaining in datamunge
//? Subtitle: A C++ implementation backlog
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

Each checkbox describes a statistical result a caller should be able to obtain. The inventory covers widely used methods and selected specialist fields. Related variants share a checkbox when they can be implemented and checked together. The three stages suggest an implementation order. For example, noncentral distribution tails support power calculations, and cluster-resampling routines support inference for longitudinal studies.

\toc

> Starting point

This inventory uses repository revision `2b265edc`. The comparison uses public C++ headers, native implementations, focused C++ tests and worked reports. A checkbox records the capability still sought at that revision. Where a formula already appears inside a plot, diagnostic, training loss or worked example, the entry specifies the additional statistical output.

The earlier `plans/stat_tests.md` lists 30 tests that already have native examples, including the exact multinomial, Barnard, Boschloo and Lilliefors procedures. Pairwise t tests, Tukey–Kramer comparisons and several p-value adjustments also have native tests. These are starting points for the new comparisons below. [Classical test inventory|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/stat_test_inventory.mepml] · [Group comparison fixtures|../tests/cpp/stats_group_comparison_tests.cpp]

Welch one-way ANOVA and Games–Howell comparisons share the same group summaries. The two-group fixture checks that the Welch F statistic equals the squared Welch t statistic. Three-group fixtures check Games–Howell against R and SciPy, including pairs with fewer than two degrees of freedom. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/welch_games_howell.mepml] · [Group comparison tests|../tests/cpp/stats_group_comparison_tests.cpp]

> Stage 1. Sample summaries and inference

>> Descriptive and robust estimation

Type 7 quantiles already appear in plots and bootstrap summaries. Quantile regression has a weighted order-statistic helper. These give direct comparison cases for a sample-summary API. [Plot implementation|../src/datamunge/plot.cpp] · [Quantile regression implementation|../src/datamunge/stats/quantile_regression.cpp]

- [x] Hyndman–Fan sample quantiles — support types 1 through 9, including their distinct conventions at jumps and endpoints. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/robust_summaries.mepml]
- [x] Weighted empirical quantiles — invert cumulative weight at requested probabilities, with defined handling of ties and zero weights. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/robust_summaries.mepml]
- [x] Weighted sample moments — estimate means, variances and covariance matrices with separate denominator conventions for frequency and analytic weights. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/sample_moments.mepml]
- [x] Harrell–Davis quantiles — estimate a quantile from beta-weighted order statistics. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/robust_summaries.mepml]
- [x] Trimmed and Winsorized means — return the estimate and Winsorized variance for a specified trimming fraction. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/robust_summaries.mepml]
- [x] Median absolute deviation — offer raw scale and normal-consistent scale around a supplied or estimated center. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/robust_summaries.mepml]
- [x] Rousseeuw–Croux Qn and Sn — estimate robust scale from pairwise distances with finite-sample corrections. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/robust_summaries.mepml#compare-robust-measures-of-spread]
- [x] Huber location and scale — fit iteratively and expose the tuning constant, scale estimate and convergence. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/robust_summaries.mepml#compare-robust-measures-of-spread]
- [x] Hodges–Lehmann location and shift — compute Walsh-average and two-sample pairwise-difference estimates with rank-based intervals. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/hodges_lehmann.mepml]
- [x] Skewness and excess kurtosis — provide both moment coefficients and their finite-sample bias corrections. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/sample_moments.mepml]
- [x] L-moments and L-moment ratios — estimate location, scale, L-skewness and L-kurtosis from ordered observations. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/sample_moments.mepml#summarize-spread-and-concentration]
- [x] Lorenz curve and Gini coefficient — return cumulative shares and inequality for weighted nonnegative observations. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/sample_moments.mepml#summarize-spread-and-concentration]

>> Probability laws and density estimation

Build on the distributions in the random module, including central t and F, beta, Weibull, Pareto and Gumbel. The plotting layer already draws a Gaussian kernel density. [Distribution functions|../include/datamunge/random/distributions.hpp] · [Density layer|../include/datamunge/plot/plot.hpp] · [SciPy statistical functions|https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/stats.html]

- [x] Binomial and hypergeometric quantiles — complete the existing mass and CDF pairs with the smallest integer attaining the requested probability. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/distribution_quantiles.mepml]
- [x] Gamma distribution tails and quantiles — expose shape/scale and stable upper-tail evaluation using the existing incomplete-gamma machinery. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/distribution_quantiles.mepml]
- [x] Negative binomial distribution — extend the Bayesian module’s mean/precision log mass with cumulative tails and integer quantiles. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/distribution_quantiles.mepml#allow-extra-variation-in-the-count]
- [x] Beta-binomial distribution — compute overdispersed count probabilities, tails and quantiles. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/distribution_quantiles.mepml#allow-extra-variation-in-the-count]
- [x] Poisson-binomial distribution — compute the count law for independent Bernoulli trials with unequal probabilities. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/distribution_quantiles.mepml#count-successes-with-unequal-probabilities]
- [x] Noncentral t distribution — evaluate tails and invert them for power calculations and standardized effects. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/distributions/noncentral_tails.mepml#invert-a-noncentral-t-reference]
- [x] Noncentral F and chi-square distributions — evaluate tail probabilities and solve for quantiles and noncentrality. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/distributions/noncentral_tails.mepml]
- [x] Multivariate normal rectangle probabilities — estimate probabilities under correlated bounds and return integration error. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/distributions/mvn_rectangle_probability.mepml]
- [x] Dirichlet and Dirichlet-multinomial laws — evaluate simplex log density and compound count mass and draw reproducible samples. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/distributions/dirichlet_laws.mepml]
- [x] Wishart and inverse-Wishart laws — provide matrix log densities and sampling with positive-definiteness checks. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/distributions/wishart_laws.mepml]
- [x] Numerical kernel density estimate — evaluate the estimate at arbitrary points and integrate over intervals independently of plotting. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/estimators/gaussian_kernel_density.mepml#evaluate-density-and-interval-mass]
- [x] Sheather–Jones and cross-validated bandwidths — choose a univariate KDE bandwidth from the observations. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/estimators/gaussian_kernel_density.mepml#select-a-bandwidth]
- [x] Boundary-corrected density estimation — fit beta kernels on a bounded interval or reflected kernels at a known boundary. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/estimators/gaussian_kernel_density.mepml#evaluate-density-and-interval-mass]
- [x] Empirical CDF confidence bands — return a numerical step function with simultaneous Dvoretzky–Kiefer–Wolfowitz bounds. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/robust_summaries.mepml#enclose-the-whole-cdf]

>> Likelihood, intervals and transformations

- [x] General maximum likelihood estimation — fit a supplied per-observation log likelihood with parameter constraints and return observed information and score contributions. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/maximum_likelihood.mepml]
- [x] Profile likelihood intervals — refit nuisance parameters at fixed values of the estimand and invert the likelihood-ratio statistic. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/profile_likelihood.mepml#invert-the-likelihood-ratio-for-a-poisson-rate-ratio]
- [x] Wald, score and likelihood-ratio tests — compare regular nested models or test specified parameter restrictions, reporting the rank of the restrictions. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/likelihood_tests.mepml]
- [x] Multivariate delta method — propagate a fitted covariance through a vector of derived quantities and return their joint covariance. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/sample_moments.mepml#propagate-uncertainty-through-a-transformation]
- [x] Fieller confidence sets — obtain an interval or an unbounded set for a ratio of correlated estimates when its denominator is uncertain. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/sample_moments.mepml#propagate-uncertainty-through-a-transformation]
- [x] Empirical likelihood for a mean — compute observation weights under the hypothesized mean and invert the likelihood-ratio test. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/empirical_likelihood_mean.mepml]
- [x] Exact quantile intervals — choose order-statistic endpoints using binomial coverage for a continuous population quantile. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/robust_summaries.mepml#put-an-interval-on-the-population-median]
- [x] Statistical tolerance limits — cover a specified population fraction at a specified confidence level using normal theory or order statistics. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/tolerance_limits.mepml]
- [x] Box–Cox and Yeo–Johnson transforms — estimate the transformation parameter by profile likelihood and return an invertible fitted transformation. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/power_transforms.mepml]

>> Group comparisons and goodness of fit

- [x] Welch one-way ANOVA — test equal means across independent groups with unequal variances and return the approximate denominator degrees of freedom. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/welch_games_howell.mepml]
- [x] Games–Howell comparisons — return simultaneous pairwise mean-difference intervals and adjusted p-values with pair-specific variances. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/welch_games_howell.mepml]
- [x] Dunnett comparisons — compare several treatments with a shared control using their joint reference distribution. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/dunnett.mepml]
- [x] Dunn comparisons — compare pooled mean ranks after Kruskal–Wallis with tie correction and the existing multiplicity options. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/welch_games_howell.mepml#compare-pooled-ranks-with-dunn-s-test]
- [x] Jonckheere–Terpstra test — test an ordered alternative across independent groups with a tie-aware reference. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/ordered_groups.mepml]
- [x] Brunner–Munzel test — estimate a relative effect and test stochastic equality when group spreads differ. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/robust_inference.mepml#compare-independent-groups]
- [x] Yuen test — compare independent trimmed means using Winsorized variances. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/robust_inference.mepml#compare-independent-groups]
- [x] Levene and Brown–Forsythe scale tests — compare absolute deviations about the group means or medians. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/welch_games_howell.mepml#compare-the-group-spreads]
- [x] Bartlett and Fligner–Killeen tests — offer parametric and rank-based tests of scale homogeneity across several groups. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/welch_games_howell.mepml#compare-the-group-spreads]
- [x] Anderson–Darling goodness of fit — calibrate against a specified continuous law and separately against a normal law with fitted parameters. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/empirical_distribution_tests.mepml#choose-the-anderson-darling-reference]
- [x] Cramér–von Mises tests — implement one-sample specified-CDF and two-independent-sample statistics and calibrations. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/empirical_distribution_tests.mepml#compare-squared-cdf-differences-with-cram-r-von-mises]
- [x] Anderson–Darling k-sample test — compare several empirical distributions with a midrank version for ties. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/empirical_distribution_tests.mepml#compare-three-samples-with-tied-values]
- [x] D’Agostino–Pearson and Jarque–Bera tests — report the skewness and kurtosis contributions to each normality test. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/robust_inference.mepml#inspect-departures-from-normality]
- [x] Hartigan dip test — test unimodality using a calibrated dip statistic. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/nonparametric/dip_test.mepml]
- [x] Energy-distance test — compare multivariate distributions with a pooled-label permutation reference. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/energy_distance.mepml]

>> Effects, agreement and categorical outcomes

The one-proportion tests already report Wilson and Clopper–Pearson intervals. The two-proportion score test reports a Wald-style difference interval. The stratified Mantel–Haenszel test already estimates a common odds ratio. [Proportion intervals|../include/datamunge/stats/proportion_test.hpp] · [Stratified tables|../include/datamunge/stats/classical_tests.hpp]

- [x] Cohen d and Hedges g — estimate standardized mean differences and noncentral-t intervals, recording the standardization used. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/standardized_effects.mepml]
- [x] Cliff’s delta — rescale the existing Mann–Whitney probability of superiority to signed dominance and add a tie-aware uncertainty interval. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/robust_inference.mepml#compare-independent-groups]
- [x] Eta squared and omega squared — report overall and partial ANOVA effect sizes from term and residual sums of squares. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/anova_measures.mepml#compare-overall-and-partial-effect-sizes]
- [x] Newcombe score interval — estimate the difference of two independent proportions using separate Wilson limits. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/count_intervals.mepml]
- [x] Risk-ratio score interval — invert a constrained two-binomial score test, including samples with zero events. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/count_intervals.mepml#estimate-a-ratio-of-two-binomial-risks]
- [x] Poisson rate inference — give exact event-rate intervals for one exposure and conditional intervals for a two-rate ratio. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/count_intervals.mepml]
- [x] Cressie–Read power divergence — support a user-selected divergence, including the likelihood-ratio G test and its limiting cases. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/categorical_tests.mepml]
- [x] Cochran–Armitage trend test — test a linear trend in proportions across ordered categories with supplied scores. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/categorical_tests.mepml]
- [x] Breslow–Day test — assess odds-ratio homogeneity across strata with the Tarone adjustment. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/table_symmetry.mepml#check-a-common-odds-ratio-across-strata]
- [x] Stuart–Maxwell and Bowker tests — test marginal homogeneity and symmetry, respectively, in a square paired table. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/table_symmetry.mepml#compare-paired-margins-and-transitions]
- [x] Cohen and Fleiss kappa — assess agreement of two raters or several raters and support ordinal weights for Cohen kappa. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/measurement_agreement.mepml#compare-ratings-with-and-without-ordinal-weights]
- [x] Intraclass correlation — report explicitly chosen one-way or two-way, consistency or agreement, single or average-measure ICCs. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/anova_measures.mepml#choose-the-intraclass-correlation-that-matches-the-rating-design]
- [x] Bland–Altman analysis — estimate paired bias and limits of agreement with uncertainty for each limit. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/measurement_agreement.mepml#compare-paired-measurements]
- [x] Lin concordance correlation — estimate agreement on the original measurement scale with an interval. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/measurement_agreement.mepml#compare-paired-measurements]
- [x] DeLong inference — estimate AUC covariance and compare two correlated ROC curves on the same cases and controls. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/roc_auc_comparison.mepml]

>> Resampling, multiplicity and prediction intervals

Ordinary bootstrap, percentile, basic, BCa and studentized intervals, delete-d jackknife, permutation tests, split conformal prediction, BH/BY, Westfall–Young and Romano–Wolf already have native entry points. The following entries change the sampling unit, calibration or error criterion. [Bootstrap|../include/datamunge/stats/bootstrap.hpp] · [Multiplicity|../include/datamunge/stats/p_adjust.hpp] · [Conformal prediction|../include/datamunge/stats/conformal.hpp]

- [x] Stratified bootstrap — resample whole rows within strata, retaining the observed stratum sizes. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/resampling/bootstrap_designs.mepml#keep-strata-and-clusters-intact]
- [x] Cluster bootstrap — sample clusters with replacement and preserve all observations belonging to each sampled cluster. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/resampling/bootstrap_designs.mepml#keep-strata-and-clusters-intact]
- [x] Moving-block and circular block bootstrap — resample fixed-length runs for dependent observations. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/resampling/bootstrap_designs.mepml#draw-blocks-from-an-ordered-series]
- [x] Stationary bootstrap — resample geometrically distributed run lengths with circular restarts. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/resampling/bootstrap_designs.mepml#draw-blocks-from-an-ordered-series]
- [x] Wild bootstrap — perturb regression residuals with Rademacher or Mammen weights for heteroscedastic inference. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/resampling/bootstrap_designs.mepml#simulate-and-refit-under-a-regression-null]
- [x] Parametric bootstrap — simulate from the fitted null, refit on each simulation and return calibrated tail probabilities. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/resampling/bootstrap_designs.mepml#simulate-and-refit-under-a-regression-null]
- [x] Balanced bootstrap — generate replicate indices with equal total appearances of every observation across replicates. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/resampling/bootstrap_designs.mepml#balance-appearances-across-replicates]
- [x] Freedman–Lane permutation — permute reduced-model residuals to test a term while retaining nuisance covariates. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/discovery_and_residual_permutation.mepml#permute-residuals-while-retaining-nuisance-effects]
- [x] Restricted randomization inference — enumerate or sample assignments within matched pairs and blocks and invert tests for a constant effect. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/resampling/restricted_randomization.mepml]
- [x] Storey q-values — estimate the null proportion and report monotone q-values with sensitivity to the tuning threshold. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/discovery_and_residual_permutation.mepml#check-sensitivity-to-the-storey-threshold]
- [x] Simultaneous true-discovery bounds — use closed testing with Simes local tests to bound the number of true discoveries in any selected subset. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/discovery_and_residual_permutation.mepml#bound-discoveries-in-a-selected-subset]
- [x] Jackknife+ and CV+ — use held-out residuals and their corresponding refitted predictors to form predictive intervals. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/conformal_calibration.mepml#pair-each-residual-with-its-own-refit]
- [x] Weighted conformal prediction — use likelihood-ratio weights and the test-point mass to calibrate under covariate shift. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/conformal_calibration.mepml#include-a-test-covariate-s-likelihood-ratio]

>> Design, power and sequential experiments

The binomial rejection and operating-characteristic tables provide a finite-sample reference for discrete designs. Noncentral distributions above support several of the continuous-outcome designs. [Binomial operating characteristics|../include/datamunge/stats/hypothesis_diagnostics.hpp]

- [x] t-test power and sample size — invert noncentral t power for one-sample, paired and pooled two-sample designs. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/design_power.mepml#find-integer-sample-sizes-for-t-tests]
- [x] One-way ANOVA power — derive noncentral F power from group means, common variance and allocation. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/design_power.mepml#compare-allocations-for-one-way-anova]
- [x] Two-proportion power — solve for sample size with unequal allocation and specified null and alternative proportions. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/design_power.mepml#plan-an-unequal-allocation-for-two-proportions]
- [x] Equivalence and noninferiority — implement two one-sided tests for means and a one-sided margin test for risk differences, with corresponding intervals. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/equivalence.mepml]
- [x] Schoenfeld event requirement — translate a target hazard ratio, allocation, power and significance level into a required event count. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/design_power.mepml#compare-proportional-hazards-event-requirements]
- [x] Cluster-randomized design power — incorporate intracluster correlation and unequal cluster sizes into design effects. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/design_power.mepml#compare-cluster-size-patterns-at-fixed-enrollment]
- [x] Factorial and fractional factorial designs — generate run matrices, defining relations and alias sets for two-level experiments. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/design/factorial_designs.mepml]
- [x] Response-surface designs — generate central composite and Box–Behnken runs and estimate stationary points of a fitted quadratic. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/design/response_surface.mepml]
- [x] D-optimal design — select runs from a candidate set by exchange and return design efficiency and estimability. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/design/d_optimal.mepml]
- [x] Group-sequential boundaries — compute efficacy boundaries and crossing probabilities for specified information looks and alpha spending. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/group_sequential.mepml]
- [x] Sequential probability ratio test — accumulate a simple-versus-simple log likelihood ratio and return the two stopping boundaries. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/sequential_inference.mepml]
- [x] Confidence sequences for bounded means — produce time-uniform intervals from a specified betting or mixture construction. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/sequential_inference.mepml]

> Stage 2. Models for structured data

>> Linear models, contrasts and diagnostics

LM already fits ordinary and weighted least squares, supplies influence diagnostics, and tests sequential terms and nested models. Extend those fits using the design, residuals and coefficient covariance. [LM contract|../include/datamunge/stats/lm.hpp] · [R stats reference|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/00Index.html]

- [x] Type II and III ANOVA — test estimable marginal term hypotheses under explicit contrast coding in unbalanced designs. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/marginal_anova.mepml#compare-type-ii-and-type-iii-term-tests]
- [x] Estimated marginal means — average model predictions on a reference grid and report contrasts with covariance and simultaneous intervals. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/estimated_marginal_means.mepml#compare-methods-at-a-common-batch-mix]
- [x] Heteroscedasticity-consistent covariance — add HC0 through HC3 for LM coefficients, including leverage corrections. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/sandwich_covariance.mepml#compare-hc0-through-hc3]
- [x] Cluster-robust covariance — compute one-way and multiway sandwich estimates and identify clusters contributing to each component. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/sandwich_covariance.mepml#combine-cluster-dimensions-by-inclusion-and-exclusion]
- [x] Newey–West covariance — estimate regression uncertainty from ordered score contributions with a specified lag window. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/sandwich_covariance.mepml#include-lagged-score-products]
- [x] Generalized least squares — fit with a supplied residual covariance and estimate structured AR(1) or compound-symmetric covariance by likelihood. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/generalized_least_squares.mepml#fit-with-a-supplied-residual-covariance]
- [x] Huber and Tukey bisquare regression — fit robust coefficients, scale and observation weights with convergence diagnostics. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/robust_regression.mepml#compare-the-weights-assigned-to-an-outlier]
- [x] Theil–Sen and repeated-median regression — estimate an outlier-resistant slope and intercept for a single predictor. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/straight_line_estimators.mepml#compare-median-based-lines-with-a-high-leverage-outlier]
- [x] Deming regression — estimate a line under measurement error in both variables with a known error-variance ratio. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/straight_line_estimators.mepml#fit-paired-measurements-with-a-known-error-ratio]
- [x] Breusch–Pagan and White tests — detect heteroscedasticity using auxiliary regressions on specified variance predictors. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/specification_tests.mepml#test-whether-variance-changes-with-a-predictor]
- [x] Durbin–Watson and Breusch–Godfrey tests — assess residual serial correlation with calibration appropriate to the fitted design. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/serial_correlation_tests.mepml#detect-serial-dependence-after-fitting-a-trend]
- [x] Ramsey RESET — test additional powers of the fitted response for evidence of functional-form misspecification. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/specification_tests.mepml#test-added-powers-of-the-fitted-response]
- [x] Nonlinear least squares inference — fit a user-specified mean curve and return parameter covariance and profile intervals. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/nonlinear_least_squares.mepml#fit-and-profile-an-exponential-decay]
- [x] Segmented regression — estimate unknown breakpoints and continuous piecewise-linear slopes with breakpoint uncertainty. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/segmented_regression.mepml#fit-a-continuous-line-with-two-breakpoints]

>> Generalized responses, smoothing and regularization

The statistical GLM fits Gaussian, binomial-logit, Poisson and inverse-link gamma models. Some count likelihoods also exist as tensor losses or Bayesian examples. The count items below call for fitted likelihood models and coefficient inference. [GLM|../include/datamunge/stats/glm.hpp] · [Tensor likelihoods|../include/datamunge/nn/glm.hpp] · [Bayesian count example|../docs/mepml/bayes/glm/negative_binomial.mepml] · [Model reference|https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/api.html]

- [x] Grouped binomial GLM — accept successes and trials per row and match the corresponding expanded Bernoulli fit. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/binomial_glm.mepml#fit-success-and-trial-counts-directly]
- [x] Binary probit and complementary log-log regression — fit alternative links with inverse-link predictions and coefficient covariance. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/binomial_glm.mepml#compare-probit-and-complementary-log-log-probabilities]
- [x] Firth logistic regression — fit the bias-reduced likelihood and return profile penalized-likelihood intervals under separation. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/firth_logistic.mepml#estimate-a-finite-effect-under-complete-separation]
- [x] Conditional logistic regression — condition on case totals within matched strata and support multiple cases per stratum. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/conditional_logistic.mepml#condition-on-the-case-count-in-each-matched-set]
- [x] Multinomial logistic regression — fit baseline-category coefficients with predicted class probabilities and joint covariance. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/multinomial_logistic.mepml#fit-two-sets-of-log-odds-against-a-baseline-class]
- [x] Ordinal regression — fit a proportional-odds cumulative link with ordered cutpoints and assess the parallel-slopes restriction. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/ordinal_regression.mepml#fit-ordered-cutpoints-and-test-parallel-slopes]
- [x] Negative binomial regression — estimate dispersion jointly with log-mean coefficients and support exposure offsets. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/negative_binomial_regression.mepml#fit-event-counts-with-an-exposure-offset]
- [x] Zero-inflated count regression — fit separate structural-zero and Poisson or negative-binomial count components. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/zero_augmented_counts.mepml#separate-structural-zeros-from-count-zeros]
- [x] Hurdle count regression — fit a zero-versus-positive model and a zero-truncated count likelihood. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/zero_augmented_counts.mepml#fit-a-zero-hurdle-and-a-truncated-count-process]
- [x] Beta regression — fit separate mean and precision predictors to continuous proportions inside the unit interval. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/beta_regression.mepml#fit-separate-mean-and-precision-predictors]
- [x] Tweedie regression — fit a compound Poisson–gamma response and profile the variance power between one and two. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/tweedie_regression.mepml#fit-zero-and-positive-costs-in-one-likelihood]
- [x] Generalized additive models with tensor-product smooths — fit anisotropic interactions with a separate penalty for each margin. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/tensor_gam.mepml#fit-a-tensor-interaction-with-two-penalties]
- [x] Isotonic regression — produce a weighted monotone least-squares fit and a step predictor using pooled adjacent violators. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/isotonic_regression.mepml#pool-observations-that-would-reverse-the-fitted-order]
- [x] Group lasso and sparse group lasso — fit grouped coefficients and expose selected groups along a regularization path. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/group_lasso.mepml#compare-group-and-within-group-selection]
- [x] Fused lasso — estimate ordered coefficients with penalized adjacent differences and return the constant segments. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/fused_lasso.mepml#fuse-neighboring-regression-coefficients]

>> Multilevel, longitudinal and panel models

The native LMM and GLMM handle random intercepts and slopes. The GLMM uses penalized quasi-likelihood with a single grouping term. [LMM|../include/datamunge/stats/lmm.hpp] · [GLMM|../include/datamunge/stats/glmm.hpp]

- [x] Crossed and nested random effects — estimate multiple variance-component blocks such as participant and item in the same fit. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/crossed_nested_effects.mepml#fit-a-variance-for-each-random-effect-block]
- [x] Likelihood-based GLMM — integrate random effects by Laplace approximation and adaptive Gauss–Hermite quadrature for a single random intercept. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/likelihood_glmm.mepml#compare-laplace-and-adaptive-gauss-hermite-fits]
- [x] Satterthwaite and Kenward–Roger inference — estimate denominator degrees of freedom and, for Kenward–Roger, adjust fixed-effect covariance. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/mixed_model_inference.mepml#compare-satterthwaite-and-kenward-roger-tests]
- [x] Generalized estimating equations — fit marginal models with independence, exchangeable and AR(1) working correlation and sandwich uncertainty. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/gee.mepml#compare-working-correlations-and-sandwich-uncertainty]
- [x] Mixed models for repeated measures — estimate visit-specific means and an unstructured within-subject covariance under incomplete follow-up. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/mmrm.mepml#estimate-visit-means-with-an-unstructured-covariance]
- [x] Repeated-measures ANOVA — test within-subject effects with Greenhouse–Geisser and Huynh–Feldt corrections. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/repeated_measures_anova.mepml#compare-the-greenhouse-geisser-and-huynh-feldt-corrections]
- [x] Two-way fixed-effects panel regression — absorb unit and time effects and provide cluster-aware inference. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/panel_fixed_effects.mepml#absorb-site-and-period-effects-with-clustered-inference]
- [x] Hausman specification test — compare fixed- and random-effects coefficients on their common estimable subspace. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/hausman_test.mepml#test-the-shared-slope-from-two-panel-models]
- [x] Arellano–Bond difference GMM — instrument differenced dynamic panels with eligible lagged levels and report serial correlation tests. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/arellano_bond.mepml#instrument-the-lagged-outcome-with-earlier-levels]

>> Multivariate, latent-variable and functional methods

PCA, FastICA, principal-axis factor analysis with varimax and LDA already exist. A full-covariance Gaussian mixture EM step also exists in the tensor library. [Factor analysis|../include/datamunge/stats/factor_analysis.hpp] · [Gaussian mixture step|../include/datamunge/nn/gaussian_mixture.hpp]

- [x] Hotelling mean tests — return one-sample, paired and two-independent-sample T-squared statistics with F reference distributions. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/hotelling_test.mepml#test-two-paired-outcomes-jointly]
- [x] Multivariate ANOVA — form hypothesis and error matrices and report Pillai, Wilks, Hotelling–Lawley and Roy criteria. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/manova.mepml#compare-groups-on-two-outcomes-together]
- [x] Canonical correlation analysis — estimate paired linear combinations, canonical correlations, loadings and sequential tests. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/canonical_correlation.mepml#find-paired-scores-that-move-together]
- [x] Partial least squares regression — implement SIMPLS for one or more responses and select components on held-out rows. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/simpls.mepml#select-components-for-two-response-regression]
- [x] Quadratic and regularized discriminant analysis — estimate class-specific covariances with controlled pooling and shrinkage. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/classification/regularized_discriminant.mepml#see-how-covariance-regularization-changes-class-probabilities]
- [x] Ledoit–Wolf and oracle-approximating shrinkage — estimate covariance and shrinkage intensity in high dimensions. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/covariance_shrinkage.mepml#lift-the-smallest-covariance-eigenvalues]
- [x] Minimum covariance determinant — return robust multivariate location, scatter and reweighted distances. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/minimum_covariance_determinant.mepml#find-a-stable-center-and-rescore-outliers]
- [x] Graphical lasso — estimate a sparse precision matrix and expose conditional-dependence edges. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/graphical_lasso.mepml#read-a-graph-from-sparse-precision]
- [x] Mardia and Henze–Zirkler tests — assess multivariate normality with dimension-aware calibration. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/multivariate_normality.mepml#compare-three-diagnostics-for-gaussianity]
- [x] Confirmatory factor analysis — estimate specified loading and residual structures by covariance fitting with explicit identification constraints. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/confirmatory_factor_analysis.mepml#fit-a-specified-loading-pattern]
- [x] Oblique factor rotation — add oblimin and promax, returning pattern, structure and factor-correlation matrices. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/oblique_factor_rotation.mepml#compare-pattern-and-structure-after-oblique-rotation]
- [x] Latent class analysis — fit finite mixtures of categorical indicators and report class probabilities and item-response probabilities. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/clustering/latent_class_analysis.mepml#estimate-class-proportions-and-item-probabilities]
- [x] Gaussian mixture model selection — compare tied, diagonal and full covariance fits by BIC across component counts, reusing the EM implementation. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/clustering/gaussian_mixture_selection.mepml#compare-component-counts-and-covariance-structures]
- [x] Functional PCA — estimate covariance eigenfunctions and subject scores from curves on a common grid. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/functional_pca.mepml#extract-weighted-eigenfunctions-and-subject-scores]
- [x] Scalar-on-function regression — estimate a smooth coefficient function with a roughness penalty using the native spline bases. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/scalar_on_function.mepml#learn-which-parts-of-a-curve-predict-the-response]

>> Clustering, model selection and validation

DBSCAN, OPTICS, k-medoids, hierarchical clustering and partition agreement already have native implementations. The graph and clustering modules provide distances and neighborhoods for several of these extensions. [Algorithm inventory|../list-of-algorithms.txt] · [Clustering diagnostics|../include/datamunge/stats/clustering_diagnostics.hpp]

- [x] Spectral clustering — partition rows of a graph Laplacian embedding and expose the eigenvalues and cluster assignments. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/clustering/spectral_clustering.mepml#find-two-communities-joined-by-a-weak-edge]
- [x] HDBSCAN — construct a mutual-reachability hierarchy, condense it by minimum cluster size and select stable clusters with explicit noise labels. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/clustering/hdbscan.mepml#track-persistent-clusters-and-leave-isolated-points-as-noise]
- [x] Mean-shift clustering — locate density modes from a specified bandwidth and assign observations to their modes. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/clustering/mean_shift.mepml#see-how-bandwidth-changes-the-recovered-modes]
- [x] BIRCH — maintain a clustering-feature tree for streaming observations and cluster the resulting leaf summaries. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/clustering/birch.mepml#compress-batches-into-cluster-features]
- [x] Silhouette analysis — report per-observation and per-cluster separation from a dissimilarity matrix, including singleton conventions. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/clustering/silhouette.mepml#find-an-observation-closer-to-a-neighboring-cluster]
- [x] Gap statistic — select cluster count by comparing within-cluster dispersion with simulated reference datasets. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/clustering/gap_statistic.mepml#compare-observed-dispersion-with-a-uniform-reference]
- [x] Consensus clustering — estimate co-assignment probabilities across subsamples and retain pairwise observation counts. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/clustering/consensus_clustering.mepml#measure-co-assignment-across-repeated-subsamples]
- [x] Nested cross-validation — tune a statistical model within each outer training fold and return held-out predictions with the selected settings. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/nested_cross_validation.mepml#generate-predictions-from-models-tuned-without-their-test-rows]
- [x] Stability selection — estimate variable-selection frequencies over subsamples at specified regularization levels. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/stability_selection.mepml]
- [x] Model-X knockoffs — generate Gaussian knockoff predictors and select variables using paired importance statistics at a target false discovery rate. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/regression/model_x_knockoffs.mepml]

>> Time series, forecasting and changes in regime

Seasonal ARIMA already fits by conditional sum of squares. Exponential smoothing, ACF/PACF, Ljung–Box, Kalman filtering and Rauch–Tung–Striebel smoothing are also present. [ARIMA|../include/datamunge/stats/arima.hpp] · [Time-series tests|../include/datamunge/stats/time_series.hpp] · [Kalman smoother|../include/datamunge/filter/kalman_filter.hpp]

- [x] Exact Gaussian ARIMA likelihood — estimate parameters from state-space innovations and integrate over missing observations. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/arima_exact_likelihood.mepml]
- [x] Dynamic regression with ARIMA errors — fit exogenous coefficients together with seasonal error parameters and forecast from future regressors. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/arima_regression.mepml]
- [x] Augmented Dickey–Fuller and Phillips–Perron tests — test a unit root under specified deterministic terms and report appropriate critical values. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/unit_root_tests.mepml]
- [x] KPSS test — test level or trend stationarity with a long-run variance estimate. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/kpss_test.mepml]
- [x] STL decomposition — return seasonal, trend and remainder components with optional robust seasonal smoothing. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/stl_decomposition.mepml]
- [x] Vector autoregression — fit lag matrices, select order, check stability and forecast with multivariate innovation covariance. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/vector_autoregression.mepml]
- [x] Granger predictability tests — test blocks of lagged coefficients conditional on the other series. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/granger_causality.mepml]
- [x] Johansen cointegration and VECM — estimate cointegration rank, long-run relations and short-run adjustment. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/johansen_vecm.mepml]
- [x] ARCH-LM and GARCH fitting — test squared-residual dependence and fit a stationary conditional-variance model with forecasts. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/arch_garch.mepml]
- [x] Structural time-series estimation — fit level, trend and seasonal disturbance variances from the state-space likelihood. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/structural_models.mepml]
- [x] Welch and multitaper spectral estimates — report spectral density on a frequency grid with uncertainty determined by segments or tapers. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/spectral_density.mepml]
- [x] Lomb–Scargle periodogram — estimate periodic power from irregular observations with a floating mean and frequency-search calibration. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/lomb_scargle.mepml]
- [x] PELT changepoint detection — return penalized optimal segment boundaries for specified change-in-mean or change-in-variance costs. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/pelt_changepoints.mepml]
- [x] Markov-switching regression — estimate regime transition probabilities and regime-specific regression and variance parameters. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/markov_switching.mepml]
- [x] Diebold–Mariano forecast comparison — test differential predictive loss with overlapping forecast horizons. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/diebold_mariano.mepml]

>> Survival, event history and competing risks

The existing right-censored API returns Kaplan–Meier estimates with Greenwood errors, Prentice scores and Cox fits with Efron or Breslow ties. Several items below share a start/stop risk-set representation with an event type and subject identifier. [Survival API|../include/datamunge/stats/survival.hpp] · [Native reference fixtures|../tests/cpp/stats_classical_tests.cpp] · [R survival reference|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/survival/html/00Index.html]

- [x] Nelson–Aalen estimation — return cumulative hazard increments, variance and interval estimates at event times. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survival/nelson_aalen.mepml]
- [x] Log-rank and Fleming–Harrington tests — accumulate weighted observed-minus-expected events over pooled risk sets for two or more groups. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survival/logrank_fleming_harrington.mepml]
- [x] Restricted mean survival time — integrate survival to a fixed horizon and estimate a between-group difference with uncertainty. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survival/restricted_mean_survival.mepml]
- [x] Delayed entry and time-varying Cox covariates — handle start/stop records with entry, exit and event ordering at tied times. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survival/cox_time_varying.mepml]
- [x] Stratified Cox regression — estimate separate baseline hazards with shared coefficients and within-stratum risk sets. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survival/stratified_cox.mepml]
- [x] Proportional hazards diagnostics — return scaled Schoenfeld residuals and term-specific and global time-trend tests. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survival/proportional_hazards_diagnostics.mepml]
- [x] Accelerated failure time regression — fit Weibull, log-normal and log-logistic survival models with censoring and time-ratio intervals. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survival/accelerated_failure_time.mepml]
- [x] Turnbull estimator — estimate a survival distribution from interval-censored records and show the intervals supporting its probability mass. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survival/turnbull_estimator.mepml]
- [x] Aalen–Johansen estimator — estimate state-transition probabilities and competing-risk cumulative incidence. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survival/aalen_johansen.mepml]
- [x] Gray test — compare cause-specific cumulative incidence across groups in the presence of competing events. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survival/gray_test.mepml]
- [x] Fine–Gray regression — fit a proportional subdistribution hazard and predict cumulative incidence for a cause. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survival/fine_gray.mepml]
- [x] Andersen–Gill recurrent-event regression — retain subject event histories and use subject-clustered uncertainty. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survival/andersen_gill.mepml]
- [x] Inverse-censoring-weighted validation — estimate time-dependent AUC and integrated Brier score over a supported evaluation horizon. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survival/survival_accuracy.mepml]

> Stage 3. Population inference and specialist methods

>> Survey sampling and missing data

Survey methods need inclusion probabilities, strata, primary sampling units and population totals as appropriate to the design. These have different roles from the precision weights in LM. [Survey estimators and designs|https://search.r-project.org/CRAN/refmans/survey/html/00Index.html]

- [x] Horvitz–Thompson and Hájek estimation — estimate population totals and means with unequal inclusion probabilities. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survey/horvitz_thompson.mepml]
- [x] Sen–Yates–Grundy variance — estimate fixed-size sampling variance from first- and second-order inclusion probabilities. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survey/sen_yates_grundy.mepml]
- [x] Taylor linearization — estimate variances for stratified multistage means, ratios and domain estimates with finite-population corrections. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survey/taylor_linearization.mepml]
- [x] Replicate-weight variance — support balanced repeated replication, Fay BRR and delete-one-primary-unit jackknife with design-specific scaling. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survey/replicate_weights.mepml]
- [x] Survey calibration and raking — adjust weights to known totals or margins and report residual imbalance. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survey/calibration.mepml]
- [x] Probability-proportional-to-size sampling — generate fixed-size samples with declared first-order inclusion probabilities. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survey/pps_sampling.mepml]
- [x] Rao–Scott tests — adjust contingency-table tests for the survey design. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survey/rao_scott.mepml]
- [x] Survey-weighted GLM — fit population regression estimating equations with design-based standard errors. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/survey/survey_glm.mepml]
- [x] Little MCAR test — compare pattern-specific means with their fitted expectations under a multivariate normal missing-data model. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/missing/little_mcar.mepml]
- [x] EM for incomplete multivariate normal data — estimate mean and covariance from observed-data likelihood across missingness patterns. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/missing/em_multivariate_normal.mepml]
- [x] Multiple imputation by chained equations — generate completed datasets using conditional fits, including predictive mean matching for numeric fields. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/missing/mice.mepml]
- [x] Rubin pooling rules — combine estimates and covariance matrices and report finite-sample degrees of freedom and fraction of missing information. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/missing/rubin_pooling.mepml]
- [x] Pattern-mixture sensitivity analysis — shift imputed outcomes by pattern and locate tipping points across declared departures from missing at random. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/missing/tipping_point.mepml]

>> Causal inference and econometrics

The causal module already estimates binary-treatment g-computation, IPW and AIPW, two-period differences in differences and instrumental effects by two-stage least squares. The items here address different designs or extend the estimator and its uncertainty. [Native causal effects|../include/datamunge/causal/effects.hpp] · [Causal fixtures|../tests/cpp/causal_inference_tests.cpp]

- [x] Propensity score matching — match within a caliper with explicit replacement rules and report balance and the matched estimand. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/causal/effects/propensity_matching.mepml]
- [x] Entropy balancing — estimate minimum-divergence weights satisfying target covariate moment constraints. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/causal/effects/entropy_balancing.mepml]
- [x] Cross-fitted AIPW — use out-of-fold nuisance predictions and return influence-function standard errors for the average treatment effect. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/causal/effects/cross_fitted_aipw.mepml]
- [x] Targeted maximum likelihood estimation — update initial outcome predictions using the propensity score and return a targeted effect and interval. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/causal/effects/tmle.mepml]
- [x] Marginal structural models — accumulate stabilized treatment and censoring weights over longitudinal histories. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/causal/effects/marginal_structural_models.mepml]
- [x] Staggered-adoption differences in differences — estimate cohort-by-time effects using eligible comparison cohorts and aggregate by event time. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/causal/effects/staggered_did.mepml]
- [x] Regression discontinuity — fit local polynomials on each side of a cutoff with robust bias-corrected inference for sharp and fuzzy designs. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/causal/effects/regression_discontinuity.mepml]
- [x] Synthetic control — estimate simplex-constrained donor weights and assess post-treatment gaps with placebo assignments. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/causal/effects/synthetic_control.mepml]
- [x] Weak-instrument-robust inference — invert Anderson–Rubin tests for structural coefficients and allow unbounded confidence sets. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/causal/effects/weak_instruments.mepml]
- [x] Generalized method of moments — fit one- and two-step moment-condition models with sandwich covariance and an overidentification test. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/inference/generalized_method_of_moments.mepml]
- [x] Mediation by g-computation — estimate natural direct and indirect effects under explicit mediator and outcome assumptions. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/causal/effects/mediation.mepml]
- [x] Rosenbaum sensitivity bounds — bound matched-study inference as the allowed hidden treatment-assignment odds ratio varies. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/causal/effects/rosenbaum_bounds.mepml]

>> Bayesian computation and posterior assessment

Build on the existing Gibbs, random-walk Metropolis, HMC, NUTS, importance sampling, INLA and nested sampling implementations. The diagnostics API already has split R-hat, effective sample size and energy BFMI. [Bayesian sampling|../include/datamunge/bayes/bayes.hpp] · [Chain diagnostics|../include/datamunge/bayes/diagnostics.hpp]

- [x] Rank-normalized folded R-hat — check between-chain location and scale discrepancies after splitting and rank normalization. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/bayes/rank_chain_diagnostics.mepml]
- [x] Bulk and tail effective sample size — report rank-based central and quantile-specific sampling efficiency. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/bayes/rank_chain_diagnostics.mepml]
- [x] Pareto-smoothed importance sampling leave-one-out — estimate pointwise predictive accuracy and report Pareto shape diagnostics. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/bayes/psis_loo.mepml]
- [x] Widely applicable information criterion — compute pointwise log predictive density and the effective parameter penalty from a draw-by-observation log-likelihood matrix. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/bayes/waic.mepml]
- [x] Bridge sampling — estimate marginal likelihood from posterior and proposal draws with overlap diagnostics. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/bayes/marginal_likelihood.mepml#compare-proposals-using-bridge-sampling]
- [x] Thermodynamic integration — integrate expected log likelihood over a temperature ladder and estimate discretization error. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/bayes/marginal_likelihood.mepml#integrate-along-the-power-posterior-ladder]
- [x] Univariate slice sampling — step out and shrink a bracket for a user-supplied scalar log density. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/bayes/slice_sampling.mepml#sample-a-scalar-density-by-expanding-and-shrinking-its-bracket]
- [x] Elliptical slice sampling — update a latent Gaussian block using its prior covariance and a likelihood callback. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/bayes/slice_sampling.mepml#update-two-correlated-gaussian-latent-variables]
- [x] Sequential Monte Carlo samplers — move particles along a tempered posterior sequence and estimate the normalizing constant. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/bayes/smc.mepml]
- [x] Automatic differentiation variational inference — fit mean-field and full-rank Gaussian approximations to transformed continuous parameters. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/bayes/advi.mepml]
- [x] Simulation-based calibration — simulate parameters and observations from a generative model and report posterior-rank diagnostics over refits. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/bayes/sbc.mepml]
- [x] Bayesian bootstrap — use Dirichlet observation weights to obtain posterior draws of a sample functional. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/resampling/bayesian_bootstrap.mepml]

>> Spatial, directional and point-process statistics

- [x] Global Moran I and Geary C — test spatial autocorrelation from a supplied weights matrix and permutation convention. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/spatial/global_autocorrelation.mepml]
- [x] Local Moran and Getis–Ord statistics — estimate location-specific association and calibrate conditional randomizations. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/spatial/local_autocorrelation.mepml]
- [x] Empirical variogram — estimate isotropic and directional semivariance by lag, retaining pair counts. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/spatial/empirical_variogram.mepml]
- [x] Variogram model fitting — estimate nugget, range and sill for spherical, exponential and Matérn models. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/spatial/variogram_fitting.mepml]
- [x] Ordinary and universal kriging — solve the variogram or covariance system with mean constraints and return prediction variance. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/spatial/kriging.mepml]
- [x] Spatial lag and spatial error regression — estimate spatial dependence and covariate effects using a weights matrix. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/spatial/spatial_regression.mepml]
- [x] Ripley K and pair correlation — estimate point-pattern interaction in a bounded observation window with edge correction. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/spatial/ripley_k.mepml]
- [x] Inhomogeneous Poisson point-process regression — fit log intensity with observation-window quadrature and predict expected counts. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/spatial/poisson_process_regression.mepml]
- [x] Hawkes process estimation — fit a self-exciting temporal point process and assess time-rescaled event residuals. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/time_series/hawkes.mepml]
- [x] Circular summaries — estimate mean direction, resultant length, circular dispersion and an interval for mean direction. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/circular/circular_summaries.mepml]
- [x] Rayleigh and Kuiper tests — assess directional uniformity with calibration for the chosen null and sample size. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/circular/uniformity_tests.mepml]
- [x] von Mises fitting — estimate concentration and mean direction by likelihood with diagnostics for a vanishing resultant. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/circular/von_mises_fitting.mepml]

>> Evidence synthesis, measurement and reliability

- [x] Common-effect meta-analysis — pool study estimates by inverse variance and return weights and a confidence interval. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/meta/common_effect.mepml]
- [x] Random-effects meta-analysis — estimate between-study variance by REML, Paule–Mandel or DerSimonian–Laird and give a prediction interval. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/meta/random_effects.mepml]
- [x] Hartung–Knapp inference — adjust uncertainty in the pooled estimate using residual heterogeneity and finite study degrees of freedom. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/meta/hartung_knapp.mepml]
- [x] Meta-regression — estimate moderator effects, residual heterogeneity and influence of individual studies. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/meta/meta_regression.mepml]
- [x] Egger regression — assess small-study effects with a specified standard-error predictor and study weighting. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/meta/egger_regression.mepml]
- [x] Network meta-analysis — combine direct and indirect treatment comparisons accounting for correlated contrasts in multi-arm studies. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/meta/network_meta_analysis.mepml]
- [x] Generalizability theory — partition crossed measurement variance and predict reliability under a changed number of raters or occasions. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/psychometrics/generalizability_theory.mepml]
- [x] Cronbach alpha and McDonald omega — estimate internal consistency and model-based reliability, with uncertainty. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/psychometrics/alpha_omega.mepml]
- [x] Rasch and two-parameter item-response models — estimate item difficulty and discrimination on an identified latent scale. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/psychometrics/rasch_2pl.mepml]
- [x] Graded response model — fit ordered item thresholds and produce category response curves and test information. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/psychometrics/graded_response.mepml]
- [x] Gage repeatability and reproducibility — decompose variation in a crossed parts-by-operators study. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/quality/gage_rr.mepml]
- [x] Process capability indices — estimate Cp, Cpk, Pp and Ppk with specified tolerance limits and within- or overall-process variance. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/quality/process_capability.mepml]
- [x] Acceptance sampling by attributes — choose sample size and acceptance count from producer and consumer risk targets. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/quality/acceptance_sampling.mepml]

>> Dependence, extremes and compositional data

- [x] Partial and semipartial correlations — estimate adjusted association and its uncertainty from covariates or a covariance matrix. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/dependence/partial_correlation.mepml]
- [x] Distance correlation — estimate distance covariance and correlation with a permutation independence test. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/dependence/distance_correlation.mepml]
- [x] Hilbert–Schmidt independence criterion — compare paired observations using centered kernel matrices and permutation calibration. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/dependence/hsic.mepml]
- [x] Tetrachoric and polychoric correlations — estimate latent Gaussian association from binary or ordinal contingency tables. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/dependence/polychoric.mepml]
- [x] Gaussian and Student t copulas — fit dependence to pseudo-observations, simulate joint ranks and report tail dependence. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/dependence/copulas.mepml]
- [x] Generalized extreme value fitting — fit block maxima by likelihood, including the Gumbel limit, and estimate return levels. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/extremes/gev.mepml]
- [x] Generalized Pareto fitting — fit threshold exceedances and report threshold sensitivity and return levels at a specified exposure rate. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/extremes/gpd.mepml]
- [x] Hill and Pickands tail-index estimation — estimate heavy-tail shape across upper-order-statistic cutoffs. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/extremes/tail_index.mepml]
- [x] Extremal index estimation — estimate clustering of extremes from inter-exceedance times. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/extremes/extremal_index.mepml]
- [x] Compositional log-ratio analysis — implement centered, additive and isometric log-ratio coordinates and Aitchison distance, with explicit zero handling. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/compositional/log_ratio.mepml]
- [x] Dirichlet regression — estimate covariate effects on simplex-valued responses with a mean/precision parameterization. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/compositional/dirichlet_regression.mepml]
- [x] Permutational MANOVA and dispersion tests — partition a distance matrix by model terms and test differences in within-group dispersion separately. [Worked example|../docs/mepml/stats/multivariate/permanova.mepml]
